course: dictionary
lesson: 11552
title: Amplify: making things bigger and louder
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To amplify something is to make it bigger or stronger. Most often it means making a sound louder, using equipment called an amplifier. «The singer plugged her guitar into the amplifier to amplify the sound.» The amplifier makes the guitar louder so the whole hall can hear it.
explain: You can also amplify messages, ideas or problems. «The news reports amplified the worry about the storm.» Nothing was made louder with a machine here. The worry was made bigger, so more people heard about it and felt it. Ask yourself: is something being made louder, bigger or more important?
explain: Amplify can also mean adding more detail to explain a statement. «The teacher amplified her answer with two examples from the book.» The examples explain her answer more fully. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "made louder" or "made bigger" should still make sense.
fact: Amplify is a verb. Its forms are amplify, amplifies, amplified and amplifying.
fact: «Amplify» can mean to make a sound louder, often with electronic equipment.
fact: An amplifier is a machine that makes sound louder.
fact: «Amplify» can also mean to make a message or problem bigger or more important.
fact: Useful phrases: amplify sound, amplify a message, amplify a problem.
exercise: «The band used big speakers to amplify the music.» What does amplify mean here? | To stop the music | To play the music more slowly | *To make the music louder | To write the music down
exercise: «The campaign helped to amplify the voices of local families.» What does amplify most likely mean? | To forget the voices | To make the voices quieter | *To make the voices heard more widely | To write the voices down on paper
exercise: What does an amplifier do? | It makes people taller | It makes paper wetter | It makes food hotter | *It makes sound louder
test: «The teacher amplified her answer with two examples.» What does amplified mean here? | Hid it from the class | Shouted it out loud | Crossed it out | *Added more detail to explain it
test: Which phrase means making a problem bigger or more important? | Amplify sound | *Amplify a problem | Amplify the rain on the window | Amplify the cupboard
exam: «The microphone and amplifier helped to amplify the speaker's words so everyone at the back could hear them.» What does amplify mean? | To turn something off | To make something quieter | *To make something louder | To write something down
